def hipsparseSgtsv2StridedBatch(object handle, int m, object dl, object d, object du, object x, int batchCount, int batchStride, object pBuffer):
    r"""Strided batch tridiagonal solver (no pivoting).

    ``hipsparseXgtsv2StridedBatch`` solves a batched tridiagonal linear system

    .. math::

       T^{i}*x^{i} = x^{i}

    where for each batch :math:`i=0\ldots` ``batchCount,`` :math:`T^{i}` is a sparse tridiagonal matrix and
    :math:`x^{i}` is a dense right-hand side vector. All of the tridiagonal matrices, :math:`T^{i}`, are
    packed one after the other into three vectors: ``dl`` for the lower diagonals, ``d`` for the main
    diagonals, and ``du`` for the upper diagonals. See below for a description of the strided
    memory pattern.

    Solving the batched tridiagonal system involves two steps. First, the user calls
    ``hipsparseSgtsv2StridedBatch_bufferSizeExt`` 
    to determine the size of the required temporary storage buffer. After this is determined, the user allocates
    this buffer and passes it to ``hipsparseSgtsv2StridedBatch`` 
    to perform the actual solve. The :math:`x^{i}` vectors, which initially stores the right-hand side values, are
    overwritten with the solution after the call to
    ``hipsparseSgtsv2StridedBatch`` .

    The strided batch routines write each batch matrix one after the other in memory. For example, consider
    the following batch matrices:

    .. math::

       \begin{bmatrix}
       t^{0}_{00} & t^{0}_{01} & 0 \\
       t^{0}_{10} & t^{0}_{11} & t^{0}_{12} \\
       0 & t^{0}_{21} & t^{0}_{22}
       \end{bmatrix}
       \begin{bmatrix}
       t^{1}_{00} & t^{1}_{01} & 0 \\
       t^{1}_{10} & t^{1}_{11} & t^{1}_{12} \\
       0 & t^{1}_{21} & t^{1}_{22}
       \end{bmatrix}
       \begin{bmatrix}
       t^{2}_{00} & t^{2}_{01} & 0 \\
       t^{2}_{10} & t^{2}_{11} & t^{2}_{12} \\
       0 & t^{2}_{21} & t^{2}_{22}
       \end{bmatrix}

    In strided format, the upper, lower, and diagonal arrays would look like:

